rther retreat what  if even the terms freedom and democracy  become taboo one day i won't compromise  on my speech  he and his group support those already  in jail they take supplies to protesters  behind bars  hong kong's future is more important  than my own future  when the political prisoners are  released in years or decades to come  i don't want them to return to a worse  hong kong a hong kong  without hong kongers  many say we are the kids chosen by the  times  but i think it's we who have chosen this  era  the symbols on his skin state his  certainty  the tattoos are messages of defiance  to his future self  and our correspondent phoebe kong joins  us now from hong kong  phoebe you have followed developments  there in hong kong for  the past year and long before of course  too  what has changed since beijing's  security law came into effect  well 117 people were arrested uh  over the past year many of them were  opposition politicians activists and  journalists the youngest one was only 15  years old  more than half of them have been  prosecuted so far and since the  implementation of the law protests have  been  almost completely deterred although the  authorities say that  the law only targets and affects an  extremely small minority as mentioned in  the report  but uh like its impact is actually much  far-reaching and reaches into  many aspects of people's everyday lives  say  we have seen a significant retreat of uh  and self-censorship among the media  apple daily was just  a pressure to shut down last week and uh  other than that the government schools  are now  restructuring the curriculums to  implement what beijing defines as  national security education and even  arts and cultural sectors  new rules have been set up to censor  movies and books from public libraries  so the law um has really largely changed  the  atmosphere on the ground that people are  getting more cautious if not fearful  about the expression since then  now amnesty international